---
// Comparison Chart band (Task 5, Course PDP Chunk A) — Figma desktop
// 675:4357 (file FX7PDNvhZwyozODaq8Q8i7; the pulled instance resolves to
// its main-component subtree at 1017:8983) / mobile 1109:14381 (the "Sole
// Switch Pro Page" > "Comparison Chart" instance inside the
// professional-courses mobile frame 1109:14374). Prop is `{ item }` (same
// shape as CourseOverview/YoullStopAndInstead), reading
// `item.pdp.comparison`.
//
// `item.pdp.comparison` = `{ columns: [c1, c2], rows: [{ label, values:
// [v1, v2] }] }` — this task's brief-mandated shape. Each `values` entry
// can be EITHER a string (rendered verbatim as cell text) OR a boolean
// (rendered as an accessible check/× glyph: a decorative `aria-hidden`
// mark plus a visually-hidden "Included"/"Not included" span). Neither
// pulled frame actually contains icon check/× marks though — both render
// every row, including the yes/no-shaped ones (Evidence Based, Continuing
// Education Credit), as literal "Yes"/"No" TEXT — so catalog.js's real data
// below uses strings throughout, Figma-verbatim, and the boolean branch is
// exercised by nothing today. It stays in the component (not deleted)
// because this repo's convention is "Figma-verbatim over invented UI": if
// a future comparison table's frame ever DOES show a check/× glyph, that
// table's data can express it as `true`/`false` without a component change.
//
// The heading ("Sole Switch VS Sole Switch Pro" on both pulled frames) is
// DERIVED from `comparison.columns` — `{columns[0]} VS` as the (mobile-only-
// recolored) prefix `<span>`, `{columns[1]}` as the tail — not hardcoded
// (review fix wave 2). An earlier version of this comment defended the
// hardcode by analogy to CourseOverview.astro's static "Course Overview"/
// "Course Concepts" section labels, but that analogy didn't hold: those are
// generic labels every course page reuses, while "Sole Switch"/"Sole Switch
// Pro" are THIS pair of courses' names, baked into a shared component that
// eight still-unbuilt course routes will also render. `columns` already
// carries both names for the table itself, so no new data field is needed —
// both pulled frames' headings are exactly `${columns[0]} VS` + a single
// literal space + `${columns[1]}` (confirmed against the built HTML), so the
// derivation reproduces both verbatim, whitespace included.
//
// ---- Component-generalization fix (Chunk B1 Task 2 follow-up) -----------
// The CTA used to be hardcoded to a static label ("View Sole Switch Pro
// Course") wired to `item.pdp.enrollHref` — correct on Sole Switch Pro's
// OWN page (where `enrollHref` IS the Pro enrollment link) but wrong on
// Sole Switch's page, whose `enrollHref` is its OWN (Basic) enrollment
// link, not a route to the Pro course this CTA is supposed to upsell.
// `item.pdp.comparison.cta = { label, href }` now supplies both, sourced
// per-item in catalog.js: Sole Switch Pro's `cta.href` reproduces its old
// `enrollHref` value byte-for-byte (same external Kajabi link, so this
// page's rendered HTML is unchanged); Sole Switch's `cta.href` instead
// points at `/courses/sole-switch-pro` (an internal upsell route, confirmed
// against Figma — the CTA's copy is a "View Sole Switch Pro Course" upsell
// on BOTH course pages, not a self-enroll link).
//
// ---- Internal-vs-external CTA fix (review fix wave 2) -------------------
// `target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"` were previously left static/
// unconditional purely to keep Sole Switch Pro's markup byte-identical —
// but that made Sole Switch's now-internal `/courses/sole-switch-pro` link
// wrongly hijack a new tab (and `rel="noopener noreferrer"` also stripped
// the same-site referrer on it). That byte-identity-vs-correctness
// trade-off was a false dilemma: `isExternal = isExternalHref(comparison.
// cta.href)` derives externality from the href itself, and the two
// attributes are spread onto the `<a>` only when `isExternal`. Sole Switch
// Pro's `cta.href` is still the external `https://…mykajabi.com/…` Kajabi
// link, so `isExternal` is true there and its markup stays byte-identical;
// Sole Switch's `cta.href` is the internal `/courses/sole-switch-pro`
// route, so `isExternal` is false and the attributes are correctly
// omitted. `isExternalHref` (`src/scripts/links.js`) is this repo's one
// shared internal/external link predicate — CourseDetails.astro's enroll
// CTA reuses the same import (final review fix wave; previously each
// component declared its own copy of the same `/^https?:/` regex).
//
// ---- Intro paragraph is per-course data (review fix wave 2) -------------
// The intro paragraph directly under the heading is `comparison.intro`, an
// OPTIONAL per-course data field — NOT a desktop/mobile divergence as an
// earlier version of this comment claimed. That claim was only ever true
// of Sole Switch Pro's OWN frame (675:4357), whose paragraph is a Dr.
// Courtney Conley bio ("...holds a Doctorate in Chiropractic Medicine...").
// Sole Switch's OWN frame (998:14860) genuinely reads a different,
// table-purpose explainer ("Trying to figure out which course is right for
// you?...") — and it does so at DESKTOP too, not just mobile; that frame
// was never actually pulled before this fix. Each course's `comparison.
// intro` is sourced Figma-verbatim from that course's own comparison node
// in catalog.js; the field is optional (the `<p>` only renders when
// present) so a future comparison table with no intro paragraph of its own
// doesn't force one. Rendered via plain `{comparison.intro}` text
// interpolation, NOT `set:html` (review fix wave 2, reverting an earlier
// version of this fix): neither course's `intro` value contains any HTML
// markup, so `set:html` bought nothing here, and the PdpAccordion/Faqs
// precedent it cited doesn't apply — those fields genuinely carry markup
// (a real `<a>` hyperlink lives in one accordion `content` value), a plain
// prose paragraph doesn't. Astro's HTML-escaping of the interpolated text
// does turn Sole Switch Pro's "bachelor's" into "bachelor&#39;s" in the
// built output, but that's a cosmetic entity-escape, not a rendering
// change (the two are visually identical in a browser) — and the pilot
// page already emits `&#39;` six times via InstructorCard.astro's plain
// `{paragraph}` bios ("two Bachelor&#39;s degrees"), so text interpolation
// here makes this paragraph consistent with the rest of the page's prose
// instead of the one outlier escaping apostrophes via raw HTML. This is a
// reference build a client dev team is porting to Shopify: `set:html`
// signals "this field carries HTML" and invites wiring it to an unescaped
// merchant-editable field, which breaks on ordinary copy (an `&` in "R&D")
// even setting XSS aside.
//
// ---- Table semantics + a11y (the point of this task) --------------------
// A real `<table>`: `<thead>` has one sr-only corner `<th scope="col">`
// (no data — Figma's top-left cell is blank) plus one `<th scope="col">`
// per `columns` entry; `<tbody>` rows each lead with `<th scope="row">`
// for the feature label and one `<td>` per value, exactly the
// `PdpAccordion.astro` size-chart table idiom (`th scope="col"`/`scope=
// "row"`, sr-only caption). `.comparison-chart__sr-only` is a byte-for-byte
// copy of `.pdp-accordion__sr-only` (this repo's established visually-
// hidden utility), reused rather than re-invented.
//
// Responsive: the mobile frame does neither of the two "obvious" table
// behaviors (a literal 2-col table that overflows into a horizontal
// scroller, or the SAME row shape with just narrower columns). It
// genuinely REFLOWS each row's internals: the feature label moves onto its
// own centered line (spanning both columns — e.g. "Continuing Education
// Credit" wraps to 2 lines there), with the two values sitting on a
// second line below as a plain 2-column split (first value white/left,
// second gold/right). Desktop keeps every row on one line (label left,
// both values right-anchored in two fixed ~175px columns, both white — no
// per-column color accent there). Reproduced with CSS Grid on `<tr>`
// (`grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr)`, the row-header spanning
// `grid-column: 1 / -1`) at mobile, switched to a flex row at
// `min-width: 1024px` — NOT literal horizontal scrolling, since the
// reflowed row already fits full width at both 342px (mobile component)
// and 390px (frame) with room to spare.
//
// Overriding `display` on `<tr>`/`<th>`/`<td>` (grid at mobile, flex at
// desktop) can strip a table's IMPLICIT accessible row/cell semantics in
// some browser/AT combinations (a well-documented CSS-table-reflow
// footgun). Every table-part element below carries an explicit ARIA role
// (`table`/`rowgroup`/`row`/`columnheader`/`rowheader`/`cell`) matching its
// implicit HTML role as a redundant safety net, so the accessibility tree
// stays correct at both breakpoints regardless of the CSS `display` in
// play — a deliberate belt-and-suspenders choice given this task's explicit
// a11y focus, not copied from any other component in this repo.
//
// ---- Tokens / literals ---------------------------------------------------
// Teal band bg = --color-teal (Figma `--bg-brand`, #047791, exact hex
// match). Heading = --type-display (700/40px) + --color-yellow (Figma
// `--text-inverse`, #fec745, exact hex match) on both frames' full
// heading text; mobile ADDITIONALLY recolors just the "Sole Switch VS"
// prefix to white (`--color-paper`) per its own pulled code (a genuine
// breakpoint-only accent difference, not a wording change) — implemented
// as a `<span>` around the prefix, white by default, overridden gold at
// `min-width: 1024px` to match desktop's uniform-gold heading.
// Intro/column-header/value text = --color-paper (Figma's `--text-on-brand`
// white) throughout, EXCEPT mobile's last (Pro) column, which Figma
// explicitly recolors --color-yellow with right-aligned text — a
// deliberate mobile-only emphasis accent (there's no side-by-side visual
// weight cue on a stacked-row mobile layout the way desktop's spacing
// provides one) — cancelled back to uniform white at desktop via the
// `:last-child` override in the `min-width: 1024px` block. Column headers
// use --type-label (600/16px); desktop's own pulled code shows 600 exactly,
// mobile's shows 700 — the ~100-weight difference is absorbed into one
// non-responsive token, same "single token across breakpoints" precedent
// CourseOverview.astro's heading/detail-label tokens set. Row labels use
// --type-h4 (500/24px) at both breakpoints too, even though mobile's own
// pulled code shows an unbound 20px — same precedent again (CourseOverview's
// h3 case). Values use --type-body-sm (400/14px), an exact match at both
// breakpoints. The divider line under each row is a literal
// `rgba(255, 255, 255, .3)` — no dedicated "divider on brand surface" token
// exists in tokens.css, and Figma's own divider is a flattened image asset
// (no fill token to read off it), so this is a reasonable approximation of
// a thin white hairline on the teal band, called out as a literal per this
// task's "literal OK if commented" allowance. Desktop's two value/header
// columns are a literal 175px each, measured off the 1200-wide frame's own
// fixed-width cells — same "literal px column" idiom YoullStopAndInstead's
// 424px panels use. The CTA button's 20px horizontal padding is a literal
// (this repo's --space scale has 16px/24px on either side of it, no exact
// 20px step) — also flagged per the same allowance.
import { isExternalHref } from '../../scripts/links.js';

const { item } = Astro.props;
const comparison = item.pdp?.comparison;
// See "Internal-vs-external CTA fix" above — externality is derived from
// the href, not hardcoded, so `target`/`rel` are only spread onto the CTA
// when it actually leaves the site.
const isExternal = comparison?.cta ? isExternalHref(comparison.cta.href) : false;
---
{comparison && (
  <section class="comparison-chart" data-pdp-id={item.id}>
    <div class="comparison-chart__inner">
      <div class="comparison-chart__intro-group">
        <h2 class="comparison-chart__heading">
          <span class="comparison-chart__heading-prefix">{comparison.columns[0]} VS</span> {comparison.columns[1]}
        </h2>
        {comparison.intro && <p class="comparison-chart__intro">{comparison.intro}</p>}
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-chart__table-wrap">
        <table class="comparison-chart__table" role="table">
          <caption class="comparison-chart__sr-only">
            {`${comparison.columns[0]} vs ${comparison.columns[1]} course comparison`}
          </caption>
          <thead role="rowgroup">
            <tr role="row">
              <th scope="col" role="columnheader" class="comparison-chart__sr-only">Feature</th>
              {comparison.columns.map((col) => (
                <th scope="col" role="columnheader">{col}</th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody role="rowgroup">
            {comparison.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr role="row">
                <th scope="row" role="rowheader">{row.label}</th>
                {row.values.map((value) => (
                  <td role="cell" class={typeof value === 'boolean' ? 'comparison-chart__cell--icon' : ''}>
                    {typeof value === 'boolean' ? (
                      <span class="comparison-chart__cell-inner">
                        <span class="comparison-chart__mark" aria-hidden="true">{value ? '✓' : '✕'}</span>
                        <span class="comparison-chart__sr-only">{value ? 'Included' : 'Not included'}</span>
                      </span>
                    ) : (
                      value
                    )}
                  </td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      {comparison.cta && (
        <a
          class="comparison-chart__cta"
          href={comparison.cta.href}
          {...(isExternal ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' } : {})}
        >
          {comparison.cta.label}
        </a>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  .comparison-chart__inner {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    background: var(--color-teal);
    padding-block: var(--space-7);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .comparison-chart__intro-group {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    width: 100%;
  }

  .comparison-chart__heading {
    font: var(--type-display);
    color: var(--color-yellow);
  }
  .comparison-chart__heading-prefix {
    color: var(--color-paper);
  }

  .comparison-chart__intro {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    max-width: 761px;
  }

  /* ---- Table ------------------------------------------------------------
     Mobile-first base: each row is a 2-col CSS Grid (label spans both
     columns on its own line, the two values sit below it) — see header
     comment for why `display`/roles are handled together. */
  .comparison-chart__table-wrap {
    width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table th,
  .comparison-chart__table td {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  .comparison-chart__sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  .comparison-chart__table thead tr,
  .comparison-chart__table tbody tr {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    /* `gap` (not just column-gap): the row-header spans both columns on row 1
       and the two values auto-flow to row 2, so the frame's 24px label->values
       vertical gap needs an explicit row-gap too. On desktop the row is
       overridden to display:flex (single line) where row-gap is inert. */
    gap: var(--space-5);
    /* literal — see header comment (no divider token in tokens.css) */
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
  }
  /* Mobile's frame draws a divider before each row and NONE after the last
     row (no trailing line before the CTA); desktop re-adds it below. */
  .comparison-chart__table tbody tr:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .comparison-chart__table thead th[scope='col'] {
    font: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table thead th[scope='col']:last-child {
    color: var(--color-yellow);
    text-align: right;
  }

  .comparison-chart__table tbody th[scope='row'] {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    font: var(--type-h4);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table tbody td {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table tbody td:last-child {
    color: var(--color-yellow);
    text-align: right;
  }
  .comparison-chart__table tbody td.comparison-chart__cell--icon {
    text-align: center;
  }
  .comparison-chart__mark {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* ---- CTA ---------------------------------------------------------- */
  .comparison-chart__cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    /* 20px literal — see header comment (--space scale has 16/24, not 20) */
    padding: var(--space-4) 20px;
    background: var(--color-paper);
    color: var(--color-teal);
    font: var(--type-button);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: transform var(--motion-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  .comparison-chart__cta:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .comparison-chart__cta:hover {
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- Desktop (Figma 675:4357, ≥1024) -------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .comparison-chart__inner {
      padding-inline: var(--space-7);
      gap: var(--space-7);
    }
    .comparison-chart__intro-group {
      gap: var(--space-6);
    }
    .comparison-chart__heading-prefix {
      color: var(--color-yellow);
    }

    .comparison-chart__table thead tr,
    .comparison-chart__table tbody tr {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      /* Minimum gap safeguard, same idiom YoullStopAndInstead's row uses —
         Figma's own row has no explicit gap (space-between plus two fixed
         175px columns handles the distribution), this only guards against
         the label ever touching the value pair on an unusually narrow
         desktop viewport. */
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }
    /* Desktop's frame DOES keep a trailing line after the last row (Course
       Price) before the CTA — mobile removed it above. */
    .comparison-chart__table tbody tr:last-child {
      border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
    }
    /* The header's leading cell is the position:absolute sr-only "Feature"
       corner — out of flex flow — so `space-between` (inherited above, correct
       for body rows whose flex:1 1 auto label pushes the values right) would
       strand the first product header at the far-left edge. Pack the two
       product headers to the right so each sits over its value column. */
    .comparison-chart__table thead tr {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }
    .comparison-chart__table tbody th[scope='row'] {
      grid-column: unset;
      flex: 1 1 auto;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .comparison-chart__table thead th[scope='col'],
    .comparison-chart__table tbody td {
      /* 175px literal — see header comment (measured off the 1200-wide
         frame's own fixed-width value columns) */
      flex: 0 0 175px;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .comparison-chart__table thead th[scope='col']:last-child,
    .comparison-chart__table tbody td:last-child {
      /* Desktop's frame has no per-column color accent — cancel mobile's
         gold "Pro" column emphasis back to uniform white. */
      color: var(--color-paper);
      text-align: center;
    }
  }
</style>
